      {
        "id": "story",
        "type": "story",
        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "The cost formula"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "A shop charges 5x squared + 3x + 2 rupees for an order of x items on a certain plan. Expressions like this are polynomials, and we often need to know when they equal zero."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "polynomial",
          "degree and types",
          "zero of a polynomial",
          "-b/a",
          "remainder = p(a)",
          "factor theorem",
          "test with p(a)"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "A polynomial in x is an expression with whole-number powers of x, like 3x squared - 5x + 7. Its degree is the highest power.\nPolynomials of degree 1, 2, 3 are called linear, quadratic and cubic. With one, two or three terms they are monomial, binomial or trinomial.\nA zero of p(x) is a number a for which p(a) = 0.\nA linear polynomial ax + b has exactly one zero, x = -b/a.\nRemainder Theorem: when p(x) is divided by (x - a), the remainder is p(a).\nFactor Theorem: (x - a) is a factor of p(x) exactly when p(a) = 0.\nSo to test whether (x - 2) divides x cubed - 8, evaluate p(2) = 8 - 8 = 0, so it does."
        }
      },
